bootstub32 - first-cut QEMU -> ELF64 Multiboot trampoline
===========================================================

Purpose
-------

Let QEMU's x86 `-kernel` direct Multiboot loader start a 32-bit shim, while
the actual kernel is an ELF64 image supplied as Multiboot module 0.

Expected QEMU layout:

    -kernel bootstub32
    -initrd kernel.elf,dominit0,other-module,...

QEMU loads bootstub32 as a Multiboot-1 kernel and creates one Multiboot
module descriptor for every comma-separated `-initrd` entry.

bootstub32 then:

    1. verifies module 0 is an x86-64 ELF
    2. loads each PT_LOAD segment to its 32-bit p_paddr
    3. removes module 0 from the Multiboot module descriptor array
    4. leaves every other module untouched
    5. jumps to the ELF entry with:
           EAX = 0x2badb002
           EBX = original Multiboot-1 info pointer

For seL4 this means the original module 1 becomes module 0, so it is seen
as the rootserver.  Modules after it remain available in the Multiboot
information.

Build
-----

    make

Useful checks:

    file bootstub32
    readelf -h bootstub32
    objdump -h bootstub32

If installed:

    grub-file --is-x86-multiboot bootstub32 && echo multiboot-ok

Example FacetOS run
-------------------

    qemu-system-x86_64 \
        -enable-kvm \
        -cpu host \
        -m 512M \
        -kernel ./bootstub32 \
        -initrd build/sdk/kernel/kernel.elf,build/facetos/dominit0/dominit0 \
        -serial stdio

Additional arbitrary modules:

    -initrd kernel.elf,dominit0,domain0.conf,initrd.tar

Module 0 is consumed by bootstub32.  Every later module is passed onward.

Limitations of this first cut
-----------------------------

- QEMU's direct x86 loader is using Multiboot 1, so this forwards MB1 rather
  than synthesizing Multiboot2.
- The real ELF must be ELF64 little-endian x86-64.
- PT_LOAD offsets, physical addresses, sizes and the entry point must fit
  below 4GiB.
- The entry point must be directly callable from 32-bit protected mode.
  This matches the seL4 x86 bootstrap arrangement being targeted here.
- PT_LOAD destinations may not overlap bootstub32 itself or the source ELF
  module.
- The stub intentionally has no general allocator, filesystem, relocation
  support, dynamic linker, paging setup or long-mode setup.
- Fatal errors are written directly to VGA text memory and then halt.
